{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788270272,"nonce":"SLE+w1pMghdzqg53Ug4xLy3Pwhk=","message":"FIXED in `dfcee33` + `f947917` + `f72f37c` (Build:), 2026-09-01. Three defects,\nnot one, and the two that were not in this issue are the ones worth reading.\n\n**1. The x axis, as filed.** `cx = x + (sw*scale)//2` added half the width to a\nposition that is already central. Confirmed exactly as diagnosed.\n\n**2. The z axis was wrong too, one cell the OPPOSITE way.**\n`cy = y + scale//2 - ((sh-1)*scale)//2` subtracts a correction that is not\nowed, so an odd-HEIGHT building's code landed one cell north. It stayed\ninvisible because the specimen this issue was filed against is a 3x1\n`ElectricStove` — height 1, so the term is zero. At most one of the two axes\ncould ever have been right, and a fix touching only `cx` would have left 1x3\nand 3x3 mislabelled with this issue closed over them.\n\n**3. The rect itself was wrong for even sizes under rotation.** The renderer\nswapped `sw`/`sh` for East/West, which is half of `Verse/GenAdj.AdjustForRotation`,\nand dropped the other half — a per-rotation centre shift applied to each axis\n**only when that axis's size is even**, with an early return for 1x1. No label\narithmetic corrects a wrong rect.\n\n\n`GenAdj.OccupiedRect` ported whole into `baseviz/render.py` as `occupied_rect`,\nand the label centred in the rect it returns — which needs no parity\nspecial-case at all. **The even-width answer the acceptance asked to have\nstated:** an even-sized building has no middle cell, so its centre is the\nBOUNDARY between the two middle ones, a whole number of cells from the rect's\nedge. That falls out of the same arithmetic rather than needing a rule.\n\n\n`map-dump` published `labels[].rot` via `Rot4.ToStringHuman()`, which is\n`\"North\".Translate()` — a **localized** string. The renderer compares it\nagainst the literals `\"East\"`/`\"West\"`. So on any non-English install the\ncomparison silently missed and every rotated building's footprint was derived\nwithout the swap. Now `ToStringWord()`, the invariant one (`f947917`, rebuilt\nin `f72f37c`). Same class as `00a1be7`: read by field, never through the\ngame's sentence builder. No behaviour change in English, where the two return\nidentical strings.\n\n\n- \"A 3x1 building's code renders on its true centre cell.\" **PROVEN** —\n  `accept/2a7c064-label-centre.py` check 2.1.\n- \"An even-width building (2x2, 4x1) is checked in the same run and is not\n  regressed — state explicitly what the correct cell is for an even width.\"\n  **PROVEN** — phase 4, five cases including two rotated, and the boundary\n  answer is stated above and in the runner's own prose.\n- \"2.5's fixture question 4 grades correctly against the published centre.\"\n  **NOT YET DEMONSTRATED**, and this issue stays open for it alone. It needs a\n  render of a real dump against 2.5's legibility fixture, which is bench work\n  and belongs to the orchestrator. Everything it depends on is proven; nobody\n  has watched it.\n\n\n`accept/2a7c064-label-centre.py` — 20 checks, 20 PASS. **No bench, no game, no\nprotocol**: pure geometry, so it runs anywhere python does, which is why it is\nworth having at all — `baseviz/` had no tests before this. Two things in it are\ndeliberate:\n\n- Phase 1 pins the port against `c718e4a`'s own worked example — a 5x2\n  `HiTechResearchBench` yields three different rects for one centre, North\n  `[C.x-2, C.z, 5, 2]`, South `[C.x-2, C.z-1, 5, 2]`, East `[C.x, C.z-2, 2, 5]`\n  — so if the port drifts, every other check is measuring the wrong thing and\n  says so loudly.\n- Phase 3 asserts the PRE-FIX formula FAILS every phase-2 case (5/5) and that\n  the two axes erred in opposite directions. A check that cannot fail against\n  the bug it was written for proves nothing, and this issue's original specimen\n  is the proof: it passed the z axis by accident for four months.","